<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06N2">Notation <span data-tag="06N2">9.3.1.13</span>.</a> Let $\kappa \leq \lambda $ be regular cardinals and let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor of $\infty $-categories. Then the composition $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\xrightarrow {F} \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}\rightarrow \operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }(\operatorname{\mathcal{D}})$ admits an $\operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }$-extension, which we will denote by $\operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }(F): \operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \rightarrow \operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }(\operatorname{\mathcal{D}})$. The functor $\operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }(F)$ is characterized (up to isomorphism) by the requirements that it is $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-finitary and that the diagram </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\ar [r] \ar [d]^{F} &amp;  \operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}) \ar [d]^{ \operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }(F) } \\ \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}\ar [r] &amp;  \operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }(\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}) } \]
</div>
<p> commutes up to isomorphism. </p>
</article>
